aa-counter - version TOOL_VERSION
============================================================

Quick Install
-------------

    curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/0.11.14/install.sh | sh   # if uv is not installed yet
    uv sync --frozen --no-dev
    ./configure

uv sync creates ./.venv with Python 3.11 and the locked dependencies:
--frozen installs exactly the versions in uv.lock, and --no-dev leaves out
the developer packages. See "Usage" to run the tool and "Installation" for a
shared install.

Introduction
------------

aa-counter counts how often one amino acid occurs in each peptide of a list.
It reads the peptides and the amino acid from a JSON file, or the peptides
from a TSV file with the amino acid given on the command line. For each
peptide, it reports the peptide, its length and the count.

aa-counter is the sample tool of the IEDB ngargparser framework. It runs no
published method, so there is no paper to cite.

Release Notes
-------------

+ v0.1 beta
  - First release. predict counts one amino acid in each peptide of a JSON
    or TSV file. preprocess splits a JSON file into one job per peptide
    length, and postprocess merges the results of those jobs.

Prerequisites
-------------

+ Linux, 64-bit.
+ uv, the Python package and project manager: https://docs.astral.sh/uv/
  uv installs Python 3.11 for the tool, so a system Python is not needed.
+ No other software: aa-counter runs no other tool.

Installation
------------

Single-user install (default)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

1. Extract the tarball into ~/iedb_tools and change into the tool folder:

    mkdir -p ~/iedb_tools
    tar -xvzf IEDB_NG_AA-COUNTER-TOOL_VERSION.tar.gz -C ~/iedb_tools
    cd ~/iedb_tools/ng_aa-counter-TOOL_VERSION

2. Install the dependencies into ./.venv, then write the .env file that the
tool reads at run time:

    uv sync --frozen --no-dev
    ./configure

Multi-user / shared install
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

uv sync keeps Python under ~/.local/share/uv/python/, so a single-user
install works only for the user who ran it. To share one install, point uv
at world-readable folders before uv sync. An admin creates /apps/uv once.

    export UV_PYTHON_INSTALL_DIR=/apps/uv/python
    export UV_CACHE_DIR=/apps/uv/cache
    export UV_PYTHON_PREFERENCE=only-managed
    umask 0022
    uv sync --frozen --no-dev
    ./configure

Usage
-----

Run the commands below from the tool folder. They start with
.venv/bin/python, the Python that uv sync set up, not uv run: uv run first
installs the developer packages a deploy leaves out, and fails in a shared
install you cannot write to. To type plain python instead, run
source .venv/bin/activate first.

List the subcommands, then the options of predict. Each subcommand takes -h.

    .venv/bin/python src/run_aa_counter.py -h
    .venv/bin/python src/run_aa_counter.py predict -h

Count the amino acid L in the sample peptides that ship with the tool.
Without -o, predict prints the result to the screen as TSV: a header line
(peptide, length, AAcount(L)), then one line per peptide. It writes no file,
so it works in a shared install you cannot write to.

    .venv/bin/python src/run_aa_counter.py predict -j src/example.json

A JSON input names the peptides and the amino acid. -o sets the output
prefix, and -f sets the format: tsv (the default) or json. The second
command below writes output/result.tsv, and creates output/ when it is
missing.

    echo '{"peptide": ["ADMGHLKY", "ELDDTLKY"], "amino_acid": "L"}' > input.json
    .venv/bin/python src/run_aa_counter.py predict -j input.json -o output/result
    .venv/bin/python src/run_aa_counter.py predict -j input.json -o output/result -f json

A TSV input starts with a header line that holds a column named peptide, in
any position, so a header of id and peptide works. Every line after it has
as many tab-separated columns as the header. predict stops with a usage error
(exit code 2) when the header has no peptide column, when a line has a
different number of columns, or when a line is empty, such as a blank line at
the end of the file.

For a TSV input, -a names the amino acid to count, as one letter of
ACDEFGHIKLMNPQRSTVWY in either case, so -a l counts L. The amino_acid of a
JSON input takes the same letters. -a works only with -t: a JSON input names
its own amino acid. predict stops with a usage error when -a is not one of
those letters, when -t comes without -a, and when -j and -a are both given.

    .venv/bin/python src/run_aa_counter.py predict -t src/example.tsv -a P

In a shared install, write the input and the output in a folder of your own,
and give their full paths, such as -j ~/aa-runs/input.json and
-o ~/aa-runs/result.

For batch runs, preprocess splits a JSON input into one job per peptide
length. It writes the jobs to job_descriptions.json in its -o folder, with
their parameter files in predict-inputs/ there. Each job's shell_cmd starts
with the path of src/run_aa_counter.py, which is not executable, so run each
command with .venv/bin/python in front. The second line below lists the
commands in jobs.txt, and the third runs them in order: 3 predict jobs, then
a postprocess job that writes output/jobs/aggregate/final-result.json.

    .venv/bin/python src/run_aa_counter.py preprocess -j src/example.json -o output/jobs
    .venv/bin/python -c 'import json; print("\n".join(j["shell_cmd"] for j in json.load(open("output/jobs/job_descriptions.json"))))' > output/jobs/jobs.txt
    xargs -L1 .venv/bin/python < output/jobs/jobs.txt

The job commands hold the paths as you gave them to preprocess, so run them
from the same folder, or give preprocess a full -o path. To merge every
result file in a folder instead, give postprocess --input-results-dir and an
output prefix. It writes JSON by default, and -f tsv writes TSV.

    .venv/bin/python src/run_aa_counter.py postprocess --input-results-dir output/jobs/predict-outputs -o output/merged
